{
  "global_card_id": "regular_divergence",
  "global_canonical": "Regular Divergence",
  "type": "merged_concept",
  "school": "Indicator Based",
  "aliases": [
    "Regular Divergences",
    "regular div"
  ],
  "definition": "A potential reversal signal where price and RSI move in opposite directions at swing extremes. Bearish regular divergence: price makes a higher high while RSI makes a lower high. Bullish regular divergence: price makes a lower low while RSI makes a higher low. Indicates momentum is weakening against the prevailing trend and a reversal may follow. Appears consistently at market tops and bottoms. Multiple consecutive regular divergences can fail before a true reversal occurs.",
  "definition_per_source": {
    "SMC-Supplement-PLv-X125": "A potential reversal signal where price and RSI move in opposite directions at swing extremes. Bearish regular divergence: price makes a higher high while RSI makes a lower high. Bullish regular divergence: price makes a lower low while RSI makes a higher low. Indicates momentum is weakening against the prevailing trend and a reversal may follow. Appears consistently at market tops and bottoms. Multiple consecutive regular divergences can fail before a true reversal occurs."
  },
  "identification_rules": [
    "For bearish regular divergence, identify at least two consecutive swing highs on price where the second high is higher than the first, and confirm the corresponding RSI peaks form a lower high.",
    "For bullish regular divergence, identify at least two consecutive swing lows on price where the second low is lower than the first, and confirm the corresponding RSI troughs form a higher low.",
    "Draw comparison lines on the highs (bearish) or lows (bullish) on both price and RSI to visually confirm the divergence.",
    "Apply the 70/30 RSI filter: check whether at least one of the two RSI points is outside the 70/30 range and the other has moved back inside, signaling significant momentum loss and increasing reversal probability.",
    "Wait for the RSI to cross below the 50 level (bearish) or above the 50 level (bullish) to confirm the directional shift.",
    "Wait for a break in market structure (a lower high after higher highs for bearish, or a higher low after lower lows for bullish) before considering entry.",
    "Draw a trend line across the swing highs (bullish) or swing lows (bearish) on the price chart and wait for price to break through the trend line while the divergence is present.",
    "Validate the divergence occurs at or near a previous support and resistance level for added confirmation.",
    "Wait for the close of a rejection candle in the reversal direction before entering; do not enter on an incomplete candle as it may wick back and produce a false breakout.",
    "Look for additional confluences such as a retest of a moving average (21 or 50 SMMA) and a candlestick reversal pattern (e.g., Three Line Strike) before entering.",
    "Confirm the divergence on a higher time frame (e.g., daily or 1-hour and above) rather than relying solely on lower time frames, as higher time frame divergences always outweigh lower time frame ones.",
    "Use RSI with period 14; optionally replace the default 70/30 bands with a single 50-level line to focus on momentum direction."
  ],
  "common_mistakes": [
    "Entering a reversal trade on the first regular divergence without waiting for RSI to lose momentum by moving from outside to within the 70/30 range.",
    "Entering immediately upon spotting the divergence without waiting for confirmation (trend line break, market structure break, RSI crossing 50), effectively fighting the existing trend.",
    "Assuming every regular divergence will lead to a full trend reversal — many only produce a brief pullback before the trend continues.",
    "Using regular divergence on lower time frames (1-minute, 5-minute) to anticipate large reversals without higher time frame confirmation, leading to trades against the dominant trend.",
    "Ignoring hidden divergences that confirm trend continuation, which can outweigh and invalidate the regular divergence reversal signal.",
    "Treating regular divergences with the same confidence as hidden divergences, when they are counter-trend and inherently more dangerous.",
    "Confusing regular divergence (reversal signal) with hidden divergence (continuation signal).",
    "Entering before the rejection candle closes — the candle could wick back, creating a false breakout.",
    "Using RSI as an overbought/oversold indicator instead of a momentum indicator when trading divergences.",
    "Trading regular divergences where both RSI points remain within the 70/30 range, ignoring the filter that requires at least one point outside the range.",
    "Ignoring the confluence of regular divergence with previous support and resistance levels, reducing accuracy.",
    "Identifying divergences on minor RSI fluctuations that barely differ, leading to low-quality signals.",
    "Not configuring divergence indicator settings to isolate the specific divergence type needed."
  ],
  "trading_implication": "Regular divergence warns of trend exhaustion and potential reversal but represents counter-trend trading, making it inherently riskier than hidden divergences. Not every divergence leads to a full reversal — price may only pull back briefly. High-probability entries require multiple confluences: divergence confirmed, RSI crossing the 50 level, trend line break, market structure break, support/resistance alignment, and a rejection candle. It also serves as a trade exit signal — bearish divergence at a key level warns long holders to close positions. Always check for opposing hidden divergences before acting.",
  "merge_notes": null,
